  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 227 PM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\n(Thursday - Saturday)\n\nThe overall synoptic pattern remains largely locked in place through \nthe end of the week into the weekend, with an omega block across \nNorth America. The mid-Mississippi Valley will remain near the \nanomalous mid/upper level ridging during this time period, moving \neastward on Thursday before shifting back to the west Friday into \nSaturday. There remains very high confidence in widespread highs \neach day in the 95-100F range, with peak heat index values of 100-\n105F. The heat advisory was extended in time areawide, and now goes \nthrough Saturday. Any backdoor front should hold off until Saturday \nnight, so barring any convection spilling into Illinois, Saturday \nwill not be any different sensibly than previous days. I will note \nthat the deterministic ECMWF does show a complex of storms moving \nsouth along/east of the Mississippi River on Saturday, but there is \nlittle support amongst LREF membership. Only ~20% of members show \nany measurable precipitation ending at 1800 UTC Saturday.\n\nAn upgrade to an extreme heat warning was also considered from St. \nLouis metro southeast into southwest Illinois due to the potential \nfor longevity (4+ days of 105+F heat index) criteria. However, each \nday is questionable for the 105+, and the time of year (early \nSeptember) is also a factor. The sun angle is lower (not as intense) \nand is analogous to early/mid April. Days are shorter and people \nhave also acclimatized to summerlike heat over the past few months. \nGiven the uncertainty mentioned above on achieving duration criteria \nalong with the extenuating factors listed above, elected to keep the \nadvisory in those areas, but we will continue to evaluate moving \nforward. Of note, there has not been a heat warning issued anywhere \nin our area within the month of September dating back to at least \n2005.\n\n\n(Saturday Night - Next Tuesday)\n\nThere have been some subtle, but potentially impactful, changes for \nthe rest of Labor Day weekend. Both the EPS/GEFS have trended \nslightly more westward with the mid/upper level ridge, which allows \nfor the backdoor cold front to move a bit more into our area most \nlikely. In addition, there has been a bit better of a signal for \nlate Sunday of potential thunderstorms moving south out of the Upper \nMidwest into our region. LREF probabilities Sunday afternoon are in \nthe 30-40% for measurable rainfall, or nearly double that of \nSaturday. The timing of this possible round of showers and \nthunderstorms though may be a bit too late to impact temperatures \ntoo much, but it does look likely that at least an increase in cloud \ncover should occur. Highs are most likely to drop into the low to \nmid 90s, though the spread is higher compared to earlier days. The \ninter-quartile range of the NBM jumps into the 5-8F range, and is \nthe difference between at least a temporary end to the 100+ heat \nindex values compared to a continuation of the heat wave.\n\nEnsemble guidance then suggests that the anomalously ridging will \nshift back eastward into the mid-Mississippi Valley, with 500-hPa \nheights approaching the 99th percentile on the NAEFS once again. \nSo, even if there is a (brief) break from the heat, it likely will \nnot last. There remains no coherent signal for a breakdown of the \nomega block across North America with the mid/upper level ridging \noscillating across the center of the CONUS through next work \nweek. At some (inevitable) point the calendar will win out and \nthe heat will break down as the days get shorter, but when \nexactly this will occur remains an open question.",
